Article 16 Where a contract violates a mandatory provision of a law or administrative regulation, and under any of the following circumstances, if imposing administrative liability or criminal liability on the actor can achieve the legislative purpose of the mandatory provision, the People's Court may, in accordance with the proviso in Paragraph 1 of Article 153 of the Civil Code that "unless such mandatory provision does not render the civil juristic act void," find that the contract is not void due to the violation of the mandatory provision:
(1) Although the mandatory provision is intended to maintain social public order, the actual performance of the contract has a significantly minor impact on social public order, and finding the contract void would lead to an unfair and unjust outcome in the case;
(2) The mandatory provision is intended to safeguard state interests such as tax revenue and land transfer fees, or the lawful interests of other civil subjects, rather than the civil rights and interests of the parties to the contract, and finding the contract valid would not affect the realization of the purpose of such provision;
(3) The mandatory provision is intended to require one party to strengthen risk control, internal management, or the like, and the other party is unable or not obliged to examine whether the contract violates the mandatory provision, and finding the contract void would cause that party to bear adverse consequences;
(4) Although one party violated the mandatory provision at the time of concluding the contract, after the formation of the contract, it had the conditions to rectify the violation but, acting contrary to the principle of good faith, failed to do so; or
(5) Other circumstances provided by laws or judicial interpretations.
Where a mandatory provision of a law or administrative regulation is intended to regulate the conduct of performance after the contract is formed, and a party requests that the contract be found void on the ground that it violates the mandatory provision, the People's Court shall not support such request, unless the performance of the contract will inevitably lead to a violation of the mandatory provision, or unless otherwise provided by laws or judicial interpretations.
If the contract is found valid in accordance with the preceding two paragraphs, but the illegal conduct of the party has not been dealt with, the People's Court shall issue a judicial suggestion to the relevant administrative department. If the party's conduct is suspected of constituting a crime, the case clues shall be transferred to the criminal investigation authorities; if it is a case of private prosecution, the parties shall be informed that they may separately institute an action before a People's Court with jurisdiction.

Article 30 The People's Court may determine the following civil subjects as third parties with a legitimate interest in performing the debt as provided in Article 524, paragraph 1 of the Civil Code:
(1) a guarantor or a third party who provides security in rem;
(2) a transferee, usufructuary, or lawful possessor of the secured property;
(3) a subsequent-ranking security interest holder on the secured property;
(4) a third party who has a legitimate right or interest in the debtor's property and such right or interest would be lost if the property is subject to compulsory enforcement;
(5) if the debtor is a legal person or unincorporated organization, its capital contributor or founder;
(6) if the debtor is a natural person, his or her close relatives; and
(7) other third parties with a legitimate interest in performing the debt.
The third party shall acquire the creditor's right against the debtor to the extent of the performance it has made on the debtor's behalf, but shall not harm the interests of the creditor.
Where a guarantor, after performing the debt on the debtor's behalf and acquiring the creditor's right, claims security rights against other guarantors, the matter shall be handled in accordance with the provisions of Articles 13, 14, and Article 18, paragraph 2 of the Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court on the Application of the Security System under the Civil Code of the People's Republic of China.

Article 34 The People's Court may determine the following rights as rights exclusively belonging to the debtor as provided in Article 535, paragraph 1 of the Civil Code:
(1) a claim for child support, alimony, or maintenance;
(2) a claim for compensation for personal injury;
(3) a claim for labor remuneration, except for the part exceeding the necessary living expenses of the debtor and its dependents;
(4) a right to claim payment of basic old-age insurance, unemployment insurance, minimum living standard allowance, and other benefits securing the basic living of the parties; and
(5) other rights exclusively belonging to the debtor.
